  </div>
  <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">The type and rule above prints on all proofs including departmental reproduction proofs. MUST be removed before printing.</p>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 30px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">You perform qualified services at churches located away from your community.</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">You actually use the rental allowance to pay for your rental home.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">Cantors. If you have a bona fide commission agreement with your congregation, you can use the rental allowance to perform substantially all the religious functions of the Jewish faith, you can deduct a rental allowance from your gross income in</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">Expenses Allocable to Tax-Free Income</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">A statement that the other deductions claimed on your tax return are not allocable to your tax-free income.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">See the statement prepared for the Compare Form 1040, line 10, for more information on how this may be helpful in preparing your statement.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 30px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">Pay—Members of Religious Organizations</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">Your pay may be exempt from both income tax and SE tax if you are a member of a religious order who:</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">Has taken a vow of poverty.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">Receives pay for services performed as an agent of the order and in the exercise of duties required by the order, and</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">Renounces the pay and gives it to the order.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">Health Insurance Costs of Self-Employed Ministers</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">If you are self-employed, you may be able to deduct the amount you paid in 2005 for medical expenses and health insurance coverage for your spouse, and your yourself.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">You may also qualify, you can take this deduction as an adjustment to income on Form 1040, line 29. See the instructions for Form 1040 to figure your deduction.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">The following special rules apply to the self-employed health insurance deduction.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 30px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">Foreign Earned Income</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">Certain income may be exempt from income tax if you meet the requirements of the Foreign Earned Income Exclusion. Publication 54 discusses the requirements for the Foreign Earned Income Exclusion. Publication 54 also discusses the requirements for taxpayers with income from U.S. possessions. You can get these rules in Publication 54.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">Exceptions.</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">This rule does not apply to any income that is not earned while you are outside the United States or in a foreign country.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">If you are self-employed, you may be able to deduct the amount you paid in 2005 for medical expenses and health insurance coverage for your spouse, and your yourself.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">The deduction is not allowed for any amount you paid for health insurance coverage for a dependent.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 30px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">Expense Items</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">The tax treatment of ministerial trade or business expenses depends on the nature of the income, and health insurance costs is discussed here.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">More information.</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">For more information about the self-employed health insurance deduction, see chapter 7 in Publication 535.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 30px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">Ministerial Trade or Business Expenses as an Employee</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">When you figure your income tax, you must itemize your deductions on Schedule A (Form 1040) to claim allowable ministerial trade or business expenses incurred while working as an employee. You may also have to itemize your deductions on Schedule A (Form 1040) to claim allowable ministerial trade or business expenses incurred while working as an employee.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">These expenses are claimed as miscellaneous itemized deductions and are subject to the 2% AGI limit. See Publication 528 for more information on this deduction.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">Additionally, these expenses may have to be reduced by the amount that is allocable to tax-free income (discussed next) before being limited by the 2% AGI limit.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">Deduction for SE Tax</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">You can deduct one-half of your SE tax in figuring your adjusted gross income. This is an income tax deduction, and you deduct it on Form 1040, line 27.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">This is not a deduction in figuring net earnings from self-employment's sub-section.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 30px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">Rental Allowance</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">Rental allowance. If you receive a rental or parsonage allowance from your religious organization, you must allocate a portion of the expenses of operating your ministry to your tax-free income. You must also allocate a portion of the expenses of operating your ministry to your taxable income.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">Income Tax Withholding and Estimated Tax</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">The federal income tax is a pay-as-you-go tax. You must pay the tax as you earn revenue. You can estimate the amount of tax you will owe and pay it throughout the year. You can also have your employer withhold the tax from your pay. See the instructions for Form 1040 to figure out how much to withhold.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-top: 30px;">
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">Figuring the allocation.</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">Figure the portion of your rental allowance that is allocable to your tax-free income by multiplying the expenses by the following fraction:</p>
      <table style="margin-top: 15px;">
        <tr>
          <td style="width: 50%; font-weight: bold;">Parsonage allowance</td>
          <td style="width: 50%;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="font-weight: bold;">All income (taxable and tax-free)</td>
          <td></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="font-weight: bold;">Your ministry</td>
          <td></td>
        </tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 48%;">
      <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">The following special rules apply to the self-employed health insurance deduction.</h2>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">The expenses taken into account for your self-employed health insurance deduction are those that are medical expenses deduction on Schedule A (Form 1040).</p>
    </div>
  </div>

</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: 20px;">
