  <p style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 30px;">How To Get Tax Help</p>

  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 30px;">
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">You can get help with unresolved tax issues, order free publications and get information from the IRS in several ways.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">You can call the IRS at 1-800-829-1040. You can also visit www.irs.gov/help. The IRS website has a wealth of information that is best for you, you will have access to tax help, publications, and more.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Advocate. If you have attempted to deal with an IRS problem unsuccessfully, you may contact a Taxpayer Advocate. The Taxpayer Advocate independently represents your interests and connects with the IRS to help resolve problems that have not been resolved by the IRS itself. While Taxpayer Advocates cannot change the tax law or make tax decisions, they can help you get the right answers and can help you get the right answers and can help you get the right answers.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Contact your Taxpayer Advocate:</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Call the Taxpayer Advocate toll-free at 1-877-777-4778.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Call, write, or fax the Taxpayer Advocate office in your area.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">(3 weeks if you filed electronically). Have your 2005 tax return ready. You will need to know your Social Security number, your filing status, and the exact amount of your refund.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Refund information. If you would like to check the status of your refund, call 1-800-829-4477 and press 1. You can also visit www.irs.gov/refunds.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Be aware that the date you filed your return does not determine when you will receive your refund. The IRS processes returns in the order they are received. If you are concerned about the status of your refund, you can call the IRS at 1-800-829-1040.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Center where you can spread out your records and talk with a representative face-to-face. No appointments are necessary. If you prefer, you can call your local center and leave a message, and they will call you back at a time that is convenient for you. To find a center near you, visit www.irs.gov/localcenters.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">Evaluating the quality of your tax help</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Mail. You can send your questions and publications to the address below. Please allow 10 business days for your request is received.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">National Distribution Center P.O. Box 4444 Atlanta, GA 30344</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Tax Products CD-ROM, and other publications.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; margin-bottom: 10px;">For more information, see Publication 1540, How To Get Help With Tax Problems.</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">You can also find information on the IRS website at www.irs.gov/help. The IRS website has a wealth of information that is best for you, you will have access to tax help, publications, and more.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Internet. You can access the IRS website 24 hours a day, 7 days a week at www.irs.gov.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">E-file your return. Find out how to e-file your return, and e-file services available from eligible tax preparers.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Check the status of your refund at www.irs.gov/refunds.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 10pt;">Where's My Refund. Be sure to wait at least 6 weeks from the date you filed your return</p>
  </div>
